o happen with  Britain's role in the European Union and  as a consequence Britain's role in the  world so you've talked about  communication versus action in your  interview with Philip a bit earlier and  you're touching upon this now as well  and he was asking whether you know in  which we're missing something about him  speaking to people's emotions and  obviously Nigel Faraj is someone who's  very good at speaking to people's  emotions why do you think he's so  successful at it and what do you think  labour should do to address that more I  think historically those who have been  able to what's now called used the  populist forum have appealed to very  basic instincts in people and they've  been able to translate those instincts  into very simple political messages and  that was undertaken by Donald Trump in  winning the presidential election it's  the kind of attitude that Nigel Farage  is adopted and not just recently but  throughout his political life and what  it says is you should be against the  establishment you should be against  those who are the the ruling class  whilst he himself is actually part of it  and has been as a broker in the  financial markets and it's a strange  paradox that Nigel crush so saw no  contradiction in marrying a German and  then campaigning to get Britain out of  Europe and being partners with and  combining the power that we should have  with Germany France Spain and the other  European nations so we're in a very  contradictory put place at the moment  where people are being appealed to for  things that actually are not in their  best interests but it's being presented  cleverly as though they are and I think  from a social democratic point of view  we've got to reconnect we've got to use  the language that people understand but  and to to not simplify the solutions  because they're not simple sort of  but to put it in a way that people can  understand and then give them real hope  for the future because I think at the  moment 