 British nation behind  those values but with policies that  unite rather than divide the United  Kingdom one of the controversies around  her is amazed and bracketed deal and had  to do with them the island Baxter and  you you've obviously worked on the Good  Friday Agreement and I was wondering  what you thought about about this and as  you were reading about it well the most  extraordinary thing of Theresa Mays  three years in the Premiership was that  she didn't go immediately to the  Republic of Ireland to talk to their tea  shop the prime minister to negotiate a  way forward that they could then have  jointly put to the remainder of the  European Union because the real European  Union will back the Republic of Ireland  why wouldn't they the Republic Island is  a member of the European Union and  intends to stay member so that's the  first thing that any incoming Prime  Minister needs to understand you've got  to get agreement with the Irish  government themselves secondly you've  then got to be able to translate that  into persuading sufficient people within  the House of Commons again without a  majority for any single party to back  that along with whatever tweaking and  changes in the deal can be reached or  you've got to put it back to the British  people  and as a former Home Secretary what did  you make of the recent kind of migration  related scandals it is the wind rush or  amber rods initiative of like making  companies disclose the nationality of  their employees and the foreign students  English competency test deputations  and the list can go on yes I think that  one of the things to resume will not  want to be remembered for in our time as  Home Secretary was the message that she  sent out through the civil service and  beyond and that was the hostile  environment because that it did  absolutely nothing to make sure we had  an ordered control of our borders and  sensible migration and Refugee programs  but he did everything to create a  attention 